# Benchmarks, 2026-11-15: programs cigcompile builds, against `cig run`, Lua, LuaJIT, CPython, Node and Rust

<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0 -->

What a program cigcompile 0.5.0 builds runs at: the 25 programs of the benchmark set, built at stage `a` and at stage `b` on cig 1.24.0 as released, timed on one machine beside `cig run` and beside the same programs written in Lua, Python, JavaScript and Rust. This is the compiled side of a run CigScript's paper reports in full, [cig-script's `docs/BENCHMARKS-2026-11-15.md`](https://github.com/otmof-ops/cig-script/blob/main/docs/BENCHMARKS-2026-11-15.md): the programs in the other languages, the rule they are held to and the harness are there. This run's raw samples are in [`bench/results/2026-11-15/`](../bench/results/2026-11-15/).

Every measured number below is made from those samples by CigScript's `bench/compare/report.py` and made again by its `bench/compare/check.py`, a second program written apart from the first to the same definitions. The two agree on every number and every character of this file: none was typed by hand.

## In short

- **A stage `b` program takes {{geomean.compute.stage-b.cig}} of `cig run`'s time** on the {{programs.compute}} compute programs, {{inverse.compute.stage-b.cig}} times as fast, and is faster on all {{n.compute.stage-b.cig}}; on the start-up programs it takes {{geomean.start.stage-b.cig}} of it. Stage `b`'s target ([#31](https://github.com/otmof-ops/cig-compile/issues/31)) is a third of the interpreter's time: not reached yet.
- **Against CPython 3.13 it takes {{geomean.compute.stage-b.python}} of the time**, {{inverse.compute.stage-b.python}} times as fast (faster on {{faster.compute.stage-b.python}} of {{n.compute.stage-b.python}}, level on {{level.compute.stage-b.python}}, slower on {{slower.compute.stage-b.python}}), and against Node 22 {{geomean.compute.stage-b.node}}, Node taking {{median.hello.node}} ms just to start.
- **Lua is faster**: a stage `b` program takes {{geomean.compute.stage-b.lua}} times Lua 5.4's time, {{geomean.compute.stage-b.luajit-off}} times LuaJIT's interpreter's and {{geomean.compute.stage-b.luajit}} times LuaJIT's with its compiler on.
- **A stage `a` program**, what a build makes by default, runs on the interpreter as `cig run` does: it takes {{geomean.compute.stage-a.cig}} of `cig run`'s time.
- **A program starts in {{median.hello.stage-b}} ms** (`hello` at stage `b`; {{median.hello.stage-a}} ms at stage `a`), where `cig run` takes {{median.hello.cig}} ms, CPython {{median.hello.python}} ms and Node {{median.hello.node}} ms.
- **cig's start-up grows with its run history** ([cig-script#252](https://github.com/otmof-ops/cig-script/issues/252)), and so does a program's: `hello` at stage `b` took {{median.pass1.hello.stage-b}} ms over the first pass, while the history grew from {{history.first.pass1}} to {{history.last.pass1}} runs, and {{median.pass2.hello.stage-b}} ms over the second ({{history.first.pass2}} to {{history.last.pass2}}).

## Against each language

A cell is the time on that row divided by the other language's: the geometric mean, over the programs both have, of the ratio of their median times. Under 1, the cig side took less time. In brackets: how many of those programs it was faster on, level with (within 3%) and slower on.

{{table:ratios-compute}}

- **`cig run`**: the script on cig's tree-walking interpreter.
- **A stage `a` program**: `cigcompile build`, the default; cig itself with the script inside, run by the same interpreter.
- **A stage `b` program**: `cigcompile build --stage b`; the script's bytecode, run on cig's VM.

